1
00:00:04,773 --> 00:00:06,427
Hi everyone, I'm Yui.

2
00:00:06,447 --> 00:00:08,995
In this one video I'll walk you through what

3
00:00:09,015 --> 00:00:11,467
Claude Code's new Projects feature is.

4
00:00:11,487 --> 00:00:12,800
Let me show off my lean muscles.

5
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:15,444
My take: if a task spans several folders,

6
00:00:15,464 --> 00:00:18,088
needs to run for a long time,

7
00:00:18,108 --> 00:00:22,200
and needs to keep running with your laptop closed, you should use Projects.

8
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:23,562
In the official demo,

9
00:00:23,582 --> 00:00:28,020
two messages make Claude start eight threads in the cloud — eight sessions.

10
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:31,080
Eight threads, fixing one website's performance problem at the same time.

11
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,587
The feature is called Claude Code Projects.

12
00:00:33,607 --> 00:00:36,813
In mid-September it went into public beta in Claude Code.

13
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:38,240
A couple of days ago the official account said:

14
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:43,100
everyone on the Pro and Max waitlist now has access to the beta.

15
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:46,360
So let's first look at what it actually is.

16
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:49,894
Without Projects, you open lots of sessions in different folders.

17
00:00:49,914 --> 00:00:54,539
For their agents to talk

18
00:00:54,559 --> 00:00:57,227
to each other you have to tell the AI yourself,

19
00:00:57,247 --> 00:01:01,743
sending it the other session's ID so it can chat

20
00:01:01,763 --> 00:01:04,689
with the AI over there,

21
00:01:04,709 --> 00:01:08,313
and you still have to hand out the work yourself. It gets messy.

22
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:10,847
Each session starts with you repeating the background.

23
00:01:10,867 --> 00:01:15,180
Then after a while you have to go check: who is done, who is waiting.

24
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,313
And then you have to wrap all of it up yourself.

25
00:01:17,333 --> 00:01:20,227
Projects hands all of that to Claude.

26
00:01:20,247 --> 00:01:23,320
A project is one ongoing conversation.

27
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:26,000
Claude acts as the overall coordinator inside it.

28
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:30,080
You drop in one big task and tell it which folders are involved,

29
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,647
and it starts a thread for it.

30
00:01:32,667 --> 00:01:35,507
It first loads all those folders,

31
00:01:35,527 --> 00:01:39,347
then makes a plan of its own,

32
00:01:39,367 --> 00:01:42,087
and gets to work, following your requirements.

33
00:01:42,107 --> 00:01:47,567
The blog's comparison is: it's like briefing a chief of staff.

34
00:01:47,587 --> 00:01:51,213
The threads I just mentioned are usually Claude Code sessions running in the cloud.

35
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,232
Claude Code and most AI tools today

36
00:01:53,252 --> 00:01:56,420
all want to push your local files up to the cloud,

37
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:59,279
so that whether your laptop is closed

38
00:01:59,299 --> 00:02:03,747
or you're on your phone, you can do live AI web coding, or whatever.

39
00:02:03,767 --> 00:02:06,101
In a cloud Claude Code session,

40
00:02:06,121 --> 00:02:08,693
it has its own branch and its own context window.

41
00:02:08,713 --> 00:02:12,633
If it needs to open a PR, it opens one itself and pushes it.

42
00:02:12,653 --> 00:02:15,520
When you close your laptop, the cloud threads keep running.

43
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:18,247
From your phone you can also check progress and send instructions.

44
00:02:18,267 --> 00:02:20,425
The name Projects —

45
00:02:20,445 --> 00:02:24,267
you may well have used it a while back, or even earlier.

46
00:02:24,287 --> 00:02:25,840
A folder,

47
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:30,175
where you can add related chats — maybe with images or videos —

48
00:02:30,195 --> 00:02:34,007
all into its knowledge base, plus a block of project instructions.

49
00:02:34,027 --> 00:02:37,267
Free users can create them too, but only up to five.

50
00:02:37,287 --> 00:02:38,795
Cowork has its own too: tasks,

51
00:02:38,815 --> 00:02:42,620
files, instructions and memory all go into one workspace.

52
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:45,984
This time Projects is a big upgrade —

53
00:02:46,004 --> 00:02:49,480
the official blog post is titled "from folder to conversation."

54
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:53,860
After the upgrade, a project is one conversation plus a crowd of threads.

55
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:56,429
It's in beta in Claude Code now, and chat

56
00:02:56,449 --> 00:02:59,580
and Cowork will open up gradually after that.

57
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:04,003
If you're on a Pro or Max plan,

58
00:03:04,023 --> 00:03:07,233
your existing projects keep working as usual,

59
00:03:07,253 --> 00:03:10,280
and the official side will upgrade them to the new Projects for you.

60
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:11,980
One difference for now:

61
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:14,463
projects in chat can be shared on Team and Enterprise,

62
00:03:14,483 --> 00:03:16,013
the business plans.

63
00:03:16,033 --> 00:03:18,447
In the upgraded version, a project belongs to just you.

64
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:19,985
The official side also attached a demo:

65
00:03:20,005 --> 00:03:23,080
a four-minute video that walks through one scenario.

66
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,507
It's about optimizing a marketing site

67
00:03:25,527 --> 00:03:29,013
whose performance has been slipping for a whole month.

68
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:33,597
In the video, they first create the project: a name,

69
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:35,547
a goal, repositories.

70
00:03:35,567 --> 00:03:40,560
The goal: get LCP under 2.5 seconds and CLS under 0.1.

71
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:44,780
LCP is when the main content shows up; CLS is how much the page jumps around.

72
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,413
Once the main conversation opens, Claude gives four suggestions.

73
00:03:48,433 --> 00:03:50,973
For example: read the repo and propose next steps.

74
00:03:50,993 --> 00:03:54,176
The video's point: you can pick one, ask a quick question,

75
00:03:54,196 --> 00:03:57,707
or just drop in a list of tasks, and it carries them out.

76
00:03:57,727 --> 00:04:00,295
The first message: paste in the metrics

77
00:04:00,315 --> 00:04:02,660
and have Claude read the code and propose a plan.

78
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:06,013
It starts one thread first, which reads both repositories.

79
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:07,540
It comes back with eight suggestions.

80
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:11,880
It adds that the first three account for most of the LCP and CLS, so start there.

81
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,880
The second message is one line: do all of them.

82
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:17,640
Claude replies: eight threads, one per task.

83
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:19,583
Optimizing images, preloading fonts,

84
00:04:19,603 --> 00:04:21,927
splitting the JS bundle, reserving space for the banner.

85
00:04:21,947 --> 00:04:25,167
Each thread reads its own code and works on its own branch.

86
00:04:25,187 --> 00:04:28,060
Open a thread card to see what it's doing, step by step.

87
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,460
You can steer it right inside the thread, or stop it.

88
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:36,327
Once a thread has a concrete change, like a bug fix, it opens a PR itself.

89
00:04:36,347 --> 00:04:38,393
Or if you just tell it to, it will.

90
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:42,234
After the PR opens it keeps watching: if CI fails it can fix it itself,

91
00:04:42,254 --> 00:04:45,047
and if there are review comments it keeps revising.

92
00:04:45,067 --> 00:04:48,070
To see every PR's status, go to the PR tab in Overview,

93
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:50,747
and you'll see them all.

94
00:04:50,767 --> 00:04:54,213
If you want to merge, just tell Claude to merge.

95
00:04:54,233 --> 00:04:56,613
After the merge, tell it one more thing: write a post-mortem.

96
00:04:56,633 --> 00:05:00,608
It starts another thread, turns the context from all those threads into a report,

97
00:05:00,628 --> 00:05:03,687
and puts it in the Library.

98
00:05:03,707 --> 00:05:06,867
The video calls the Library the project's shared folder.

99
00:05:06,887 --> 00:05:08,183
Every thread can read it,

100
00:05:08,203 --> 00:05:11,580
so next time it can optimize again and simply update the report.

101
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:14,165
The last step: connect a monitoring service

102
00:05:14,185 --> 00:05:17,580
to automate it further — Sentry, in the demo.

103
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:22,007
Then have it create a routine — a scheduled task.

104
00:05:22,027 --> 00:05:24,747
If it finds a regression, it starts a thread to investigate on its own.

105
00:05:24,767 --> 00:05:27,020
Next time you open the project, it's already waiting for you.

106
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:29,363
The routine feature itself is labeled research

107
00:05:29,383 --> 00:05:31,480
preview by the official side.

108
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,440
Behavior and limits may change.

109
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:35,400
In the demo it actually finds one:

110
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:40,540
on the pricing page, mobile LCP is 3.4 seconds, up from 2.1 the day before.

111
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:45,067
The cause is a 1.9 MB PNG slowing down loading.

112
00:05:45,087 --> 00:05:47,537
So how do you get started? Open Claude Code

113
00:05:47,557 --> 00:05:50,080
and you should see Projects in your left sidebar.

114
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:53,563
You can also open the web version

115
00:05:53,583 --> 00:05:55,880
at claude.ai/code.

116
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:57,313
The mobile app has it too.

117
00:05:57,333 --> 00:06:00,800
If you don't see it, it just hasn't reached you yet.

118
00:06:00,820 --> 00:06:04,260
The official side has a waitlist, and I'll put the link in the description.

119
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:05,551
If you don't have access yet,

120
00:06:05,571 --> 00:06:09,753
check the official page: it lists five ways to run work in parallel.

121
00:06:09,773 --> 00:06:11,580
Step two: create a project.

122
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,013
Click Projects and a dialog asks for its name.

123
00:06:15,033 --> 00:06:16,473
Only the name is required.

124
00:06:16,493 --> 00:06:20,213
Write the goal in one line; repositories and files can be added later.

125
00:06:20,233 --> 00:06:22,060
For code projects there are three prerequisites:

126
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:24,447
Your code must be on GitHub so it can be linked.

127
00:06:24,467 --> 00:06:27,927
Your account needs push access, and the Claude GitHub App must be installed.

128
00:06:27,947 --> 00:06:31,847
GitLab and Bitbucket don't work; for now it only supports GitHub.

129
00:06:31,867 --> 00:06:34,060
And GitHub Enterprise Server doesn't work either.

130
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,509
No repository is fine too: do research, write articles,

131
00:06:36,529 --> 00:06:38,680
and put the results in the Library.

132
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:42,280
Step three is writing the project instructions, up to 16,000 characters.

133
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:43,920
The docs suggest writing five things:

134
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:47,300
what the project is for, which branch to use, how to check the work,

135
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:50,000
what to do when something is missing, and what to ask you first.

136
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:53,193
One line in the official example: if something is missing, stop and say exactly what.

137
00:06:53,213 --> 00:06:55,480
No substituting, no mocking, no guessing.

138
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:57,161
Merging, force-pushing and changing CI config all need to wait

139
00:06:57,181 --> 00:06:59,480
for your answer in the thread first.

140
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:02,427
Step four: send one real task first.

141
00:07:02,447 --> 00:07:05,920
See how it reports back, then send in your whole batch.

142
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:09,000
One more thing: after your first project is created,

143
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:10,511
if you haven't sent a message,

144
00:07:10,531 --> 00:07:13,320
Claude Code takes a turn on its own to get a feel for it.

145
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:15,727
That turn uses your plan too.

146
00:07:15,747 --> 00:07:19,307
The Library takes up to 100 files and 2 GB at a time.

147
00:07:19,327 --> 00:07:21,667
A single file can be at most 480 MB.

148
00:07:21,687 --> 00:07:25,693
Files over 30 MB are skipped when you create the project; upload them after.

149
00:07:25,713 --> 00:07:28,173
At the end of the video the official side gives three tips.

150
00:07:28,193 --> 00:07:30,847
Tip one: keep your usage under control.

151
00:07:30,867 --> 00:07:33,713
Every thread is a full Claude Code session.

152
00:07:33,733 --> 00:07:36,847
Run several at once and your plan drains very fast.

153
00:07:36,867 --> 00:07:38,840
A new project runs Opus everywhere by default.

154
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:42,647
The docs say threads at high effort and the coordinator at low.

155
00:07:42,667 --> 00:07:46,567
But in the demo's settings page, threads show Medium.

156
00:07:46,587 --> 00:07:48,213
Go by what you see on your own screen.

157
00:07:48,233 --> 00:07:52,073
The advice: leave the coordinator on low effort.

158
00:07:52,093 --> 00:07:57,107
It mostly routes tasks, so turning it up isn't worth much.

159
00:07:57,127 --> 00:08:02,027
Threads can default to a smaller model; switch to a better one for bigger tasks.

160
00:08:02,047 --> 00:08:05,073
In the demo they switch the thread model to Sonnet.

161
00:08:05,093 --> 00:08:07,660
Tip two: tell Claude how you want it to work.

162
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:10,640
How many threads at once, how often to report back.

163
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:13,453
Tip three: threads share memory.

164
00:08:13,473 --> 00:08:17,113
Tell it your preferences first, and every later thread will read them.

165
00:08:17,133 --> 00:08:20,253
In settings you can see it, edit it, and delete it.

166
00:08:20,273 --> 00:08:22,592
It's separate from your local CLAUDE.md

167
00:08:22,612 --> 00:08:27,133
and your machine's auto-memory MEMORY.md files.

168
00:08:27,153 --> 00:08:29,760
The docs have a few more details that are easy to miss.

169
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:33,120
For example, when a thread needs your approval, you have to go into the thread to approve.

170
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:36,680
Saying yes in the project conversation doesn't reach it.

171
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:40,613
You have to open the thread and click the Allow button.

172
00:08:40,633 --> 00:08:43,213
In the Overview pane, threads are sorted into six groups:

173
00:08:43,233 --> 00:08:47,299
waiting for your review, waiting for your reply, running,

174
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:51,073
then landing, idle, and finally resolved.

175
00:08:51,093 --> 00:08:55,047
A thread with no activity for a week is archived automatically as resolved.

176
00:08:55,067 --> 00:08:58,813
If you'd rather not go to the cloud, you can also run it locally.

177
00:08:58,833 --> 00:09:02,067
Pick Work Locally from the plus menu.

178
00:09:02,087 --> 00:09:05,133
It has a version requirement, and your computer has to stay on.

179
00:09:05,153 --> 00:09:08,687
Cloud threads don't inherit anything installed on your machine.

180
00:09:08,707 --> 00:09:10,465
To use connectors, connect them

181
00:09:10,485 --> 00:09:13,120
to your Claude.ai account — you have to log in first.

182
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:14,323
And skills and subagents

183
00:09:14,343 --> 00:09:18,540
need to be committed to the .claude hidden folder in your cloud repository first.

184
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:21,247
So when is Projects worth using?

185
00:09:21,267 --> 00:09:25,000
For this kind of task, rather than just working in a local folder:

186
00:09:25,020 --> 00:09:28,739
say your goal is bigger than one session,

187
00:09:28,759 --> 00:09:32,380
spans several folders, and needs lots of tasks in parallel.

188
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:35,580
For example, migrating an app off a deprecated package,

189
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:38,705
or changing one endpoint across the API, web and mobile repos —

190
00:09:38,725 --> 00:09:41,193
that's when you use Projects.

191
00:09:41,213 --> 00:09:44,873
Claude Code now has five ways to do many things at once:

192
00:09:44,893 --> 00:09:47,480
subagents, agent view, agent teams,

193
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:48,553
dynamic workflows,

194
00:09:48,573 --> 00:09:51,080
plus the Projects that just came out.

195
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:52,880
You have plenty of options.

196
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:55,380
The official positioning of Projects is quite specific:

197
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:57,676
work with many tasks, lasting weeks,

198
00:09:57,696 --> 00:10:01,747
that has to keep running with your computer off — that's when you use it.

199
00:10:01,767 --> 00:10:05,012
And you want to describe it once, not repeat your requirements

200
00:10:05,032 --> 00:10:08,853
and preferences in every new window, and not watch each one.

201
00:10:08,873 --> 00:10:12,193
Agent teams is still labeled experimental.

202
00:10:12,213 --> 00:10:14,700
Projects is in public beta.

203
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:18,887
Conversely, when shouldn't you use Projects? There are four cases:

204
00:10:18,907 --> 00:10:22,780
If one session can finish the job, you don't need Projects.

205
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:26,300
If every task needs your local files,

206
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:27,907
run it locally.

207
00:10:27,927 --> 00:10:30,200
If it's a single scheduled task

208
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:33,113
with no conversation, a routine is enough.

209
00:10:33,133 --> 00:10:38,701
If a group of people want to steer it together in Slack,

210
00:10:38,721 --> 00:10:42,525
install Claude Tag in Slack —

211
00:10:42,545 --> 00:10:45,247
you don't need Projects.

212
00:10:45,267 --> 00:10:47,687
Its usage cost is high,

213
00:10:47,707 --> 00:10:50,580
Projects burns through your plan faster than a single session.

214
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:53,260
So — should you use Projects?

215
00:10:53,280 --> 00:10:57,327
There's only one test: is your task longer than a single session?

216
00:10:57,347 --> 00:11:00,431
If your session is long, spans lots of folders,

217
00:11:00,451 --> 00:11:02,787
may take days or weeks, and tasks keep coming,

218
00:11:02,807 --> 00:11:05,840
and you want it to keep running in the cloud even after you've left work

219
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:07,547
with your laptop in transit,

220
00:11:07,567 --> 00:11:09,600
then it's well worth starting a project.

221
00:11:09,620 --> 00:11:12,390
Alright, that's it for this video. Thanks for watching.
